@*
    One page of the Settings screen, in a file of its own so a change to this
    page is a change to this file. The screen (Settings.razor) keeps the menu,
    the headline and the panel this is drawn in; what is on the page, and what it
    writes, is here.

    The namespace is the screen's rather than the folder's, and this folder has
    no _Imports.razor, for the reason the note at the top of Settings.razor
    gives.
*@
@namespace Backlog.Desktop.UI.Shell
@inherits SettingsPageBase
@using System.Globalization
@using Backlog.Infrastructure.FileSystem
@using Backlog.SharedKernel
@using Backlog.Modules.Devbook.Abstractions
@* SelectField draws the backup schedule; written here because this folder
   cannot have an _Imports of its own. *@
@using Backlog.UI.Components.Selects
@* MetricGrid and MetricTile draw the Backup card's two figures. *@
@using Backlog.UI.Components.Metrics
@* The Brain card, drawn by the library and fed here. *@
@using Backlog.UI.Components.Brain
@* For the optional logger a missing backup worker is noted in, rather than
   failing the page over it. *@
@using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging
@inject WorkspaceSettingsStore Store
@* The optional collaborators - the backup worker and the Brain's setting -
   are read off this rather than injected, so a host that composes none of
   them can still render the page. *@
@inject IServiceProvider Services
@implements IDisposable

@* The page's sentence in the frame's headline: is the backlog safe? Re-read
   on every render, so a backup that completes while the page is open - the
   worker's Changed redraws it - moves it. *@
<SettingsHeadline><span data-testid="storage-headline">@Headline</span></SettingsHeadline>

@* Two cards side by side, wrapping to one column on a narrow window: where
   the backlog is and where it is backed up to. The working week that was a
   third is the Dashboard's page now, and repositories and GitHub accounts
   are pages of their own. The branch snapshot folder sits in the first card
   because it follows the folder by default. The titles are what
   SettingsPages declares for the settings search. *@
<div class="setting-columns storage-cards">
    <SettingsCard Title="Where the backlog is stored"
                  Id="storage-folder-title"
                  TestId="storage-folder-settings">
        @* This paragraph used to say every entry was its own markdown file
           and to suggest pointing the folder at a synced one. Both halves
           stopped being true when the store became one database, and the
           suggestion cost somebody their edits - see R9 in
           .devbook/arc42/11-risks-and-technical-debt.md. The warning is the copy
           now, and SettingsStorageCopyTests pins it. *@
        <p class="setting__body" data-testid="storage-path-description">
            The whole backlog is one SQLite database in this folder. Your text stays
            markdown inside it, but it is a single binary file - so keep this on a local
            disk. A file-sync folder like OneDrive or Dropbox cannot merge it, and two
            machines writing it will lose edits.
        </p>

        @* The concrete half of the paragraph above. That one can say
           what a synced folder does to the database; only this can say
           that the folder in the field is one - which is the gap local
           ADR 0005 left the copy holding, because a root that followed
           the retired advice stays where it is. It names the provider
           and stops there: the root is the reader's choice and this
           warns rather than prevents. Renders nothing when the store
           recognised nothing, which is also what an unknown machine
           and an unreadable disk produce. *@
        <Alert Message="@SyncedRootWarning"
               BaseClass="setting__warning"
               Id="storage-path-sync-warning"
               TestId="storage-path-sync-warning" />

        @* The error class goes on InputCssClass, not ErrorMessage: this
           page reports the reason in its own status paragraph below, and
           ErrorMessage would render a second one as an Alert.

           No @onchange, and deliberately. The field used to commit a move
           on change, which fires on blur - so clicking "Switch without
           moving" after typing a folder moved the backlog there first and
           then switched to it, and the button could never do what it said.
           Enter still moves, because a key pressed on purpose is a choice;
           leaving the field is not.

           Browse fills the field and commits nothing either, for the same
           reason: which of the two buttons below applies the folder is still
           the reader's choice. Drawn only where the host has a folder picker. *@
        <div class="storage-folder">
            <TextField Bare="true"
                       LabelCssClass="setting__label"
                       InputCssClass="@($"{SettingsScreenContext.SettingInputClass(_error)} setting__input--mono")"
                       Label="Folder"
                       Id="storage-path"
                       data-testid="storage-path-input"
                       spellcheck="false"
                       autocomplete="off"
                       aria-describedby="@StoragePathDescribedBy"
                       Value="@_draftPath"
                       ValueChanged="value => _draftPath = value"
                       @onkeydown="OnKeyDownAsync" />
            @if (_brainPicker is not null)
            {
                <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary"
                           TestId="storage-path-browse"
                           Busy="@_browsingStoragePath"
                           BusyLabel="Choosing a folder"
                           OnClick="BrowseStoragePathAsync">
                    Browse
                </AppButton>
            }
        </div>

        <p class="setting__status" id="storage-path-status" data-testid="storage-path-status" role="status">
            @if (_error is not null)
            {
                <span class="setting__status--error">@_error</span>
            }
            else if (_moved && _copied)
            {
                <span class="setting__status--ok">Moved. The backlog was copied here and now loads from this folder; the copy in <code>@_previousRoot</code> was left as it was.</span>
            }
            else if (_moved)
            {
                <span class="setting__status--ok">Switched. Entries now load from this folder - nothing was copied.</span>
            }
            else
            {
                <span>Tasks live in <code>@Store.DatabasePath</code>@(Store.IsDefaultRoot ? " - the default location." : ".") Inbox is prepared at <code>@Store.InboxDirectory</code>.</span>
            }
        </p>

        @* Two ways to leave a folder, each a button, plus the default
           folder as the move it most often is. Moving takes the backlog
           along; switching leaves it behind and reads whatever the other
           folder holds. Switching used to be the only way, which is how
           "Use the default folder" came to empty a backlog whose database
           was still sitting on OneDrive - and moving used to be only the
           default button and the field's own change event, so the folder
           somebody typed had no button that said "move" over it. *@
        <div class="setting__actions">
            @* The pressed one is busy while the folder changes. The other two
               say they are blocked and drop a press meanwhile, because a second
               change of folder under the first is not a thing to start - but
               they keep their place in the tab order, as a busy button does. *@
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.None"
                       data-testid="move-storage-path"
                       Disabled="@RootButtonDisabled(RootAct.Move, IsCurrentRootDraft)"
                       Busy="@(_rootAct is RootAct.Move)"
                       BusyLabel="Moving"
                       aria-disabled="@RootButtonsBlocked"
                       OnClick="ApplyAsync">
                Move to this folder
            </AppButton>
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.None"
                       data-testid="switch-storage-path"
                       Disabled="@RootButtonDisabled(RootAct.Switch, IsCurrentRootDraft)"
                       Busy="@(_rootAct is RootAct.Switch)"
                       BusyLabel="Switching"
                       aria-disabled="@RootButtonsBlocked"
                       OnClick="SwitchAsync">
                Switch without moving
            </AppButton>
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.None"
                       data-testid="reset-storage-path"
                       Disabled="@RootButtonDisabled(RootAct.Default, Store.IsDefaultRoot)"
                       Busy="@(_rootAct is RootAct.Default)"
                       BusyLabel="Moving"
                       aria-disabled="@RootButtonsBlocked"
                       OnClick="MoveToDefaultAsync">
                Move to the default folder
            </AppButton>
        </div>

        <p class="setting__note">
            Moving takes the backlog along: the database, the inbox folder and the
            app's own configuration are copied there, and the app reads from the
            new folder from then on. Anything else you keep in the folder stays where
            it is, and so does the old copy - delete it yourself once you are sure. A
            folder that already holds a backlog is never written over. Switch without
            moving to open a backlog that already lives in the folder you entered; that
            copies nothing.
        </p>


        @* Under the storage folder by default, so a backlog moved to
           another disk takes the default location of what was fetched
           to read beside it along. It is a setting rather than a
           constant because a machine with a small system drive and a
           large one for work is an ordinary machine. Not backed up and
           not moved: a snapshot is a disposable copy of a commit that
           can always be fetched again. *@
        <div class="devbook-folders" data-testid="devbook-cache-settings">
            <h3 class="devbook-folders__title">Branch snapshot folder</h3>
            <p class="setting__note">
                Where the devbook fetched from a repository branch is cached. It sits under the
                storage folder unless you point it elsewhere; clear the field to return it there.
                Changing this leaves the old folder alone; the new one refills on the next fetch.
            </p>
            <TextField Bare="true"
                       LabelCssClass="setting__label"
                       InputCssClass="@($"{SettingsScreenContext.SettingInputClass(_devbookCacheError)} setting__input--mono")"
                       Label="Snapshot folder"
                       Id="devbook-cache-directory"
                       data-testid="devbook-cache-directory-input"
                       spellcheck="false"
                       autocomplete="off"
                       Placeholder="@Store.DefaultDevbookCacheDirectory"
                       aria-describedby="devbook-cache-status"
                       Value="@_draftDevbookCacheDirectory"
                       ValueChanged="value => _draftDevbookCacheDirectory = value"
                       @onchange="ApplyDevbookCacheDirectory"
                       @onkeydown="OnDevbookCacheDirectoryKeyDown" />
            <p class="setting__status" id="devbook-cache-status" data-testid="devbook-cache-status" role="status">
                @if (_devbookCacheError is not null)
                {
                    <span class="setting__status--error">@_devbookCacheError</span>
                }
                else
                {
                    <span>Snapshots: <code>@Store.DevbookCacheDirectory</code>@(Store.IsDefaultDevbookCacheDirectory ? " - under the storage folder." : ".")</span>
                }
            </p>
        </div>
    </SettingsCard>

    @* One-way, and the copy says so twice: a backup is a copy that
       leaves, and nothing here ever reads the repository back into
       the folder. A backup that came home on its own would be file
       sync by another name, and R9 in .devbook/arc42/11-risks-and-technical-debt.md
       is what that did to this database. Local ADR 0010 is the
       decision; BackupWorker is the mechanism. *@
    <SettingsCard Title="Backup"
                  Id="storage-backup-title"
                  TestId="storage-backup-settings">
        <Action>
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Primary"
                       TestId="storage-backup-now"
                       Busy="@(_backup?.IsRunning ?? false)"
                       BusyLabel="Backing up"
                       Disabled="@(_backup is null || Store.RootRepository is null)"
                       OnClick="BackUpNow">
                Back up now
            </AppButton>
        </Action>
        <ChildContent>
        @* The two figures a reader checks first, over the line that says what
           the last run did. A machine that backed up before the copies were
           counted keeps at least the one it has. *@
        <MetricGrid Label="Backup figures" MinColumnWidth="10rem" CssClass="storage-backup__figures" TestId="storage-backup-figures">
            <MetricTile Label="Last backup"
                        Value="@LastBackupFigure"
                        TestId="storage-backup-last" />
            <MetricTile Label="Keeping"
                        Value="@DailyCopies.ToString(CultureInfo.CurrentCulture)"
                        Unit="@(DailyCopies == 1 ? "daily copy" : "daily copies")"
                        TestId="storage-backup-copies" />
        </MetricGrid>

        <p class="setting__status @(BackupStatusIsFailure ? "setting__status--error" : null)"
           id="storage-backup-status"
           data-testid="storage-backup-status"
           role="@(BackupStatusIsFailure ? "alert" : "status")">
            <span>@BackupStatusLine</span>
        </p>

        <p class="setting__body">
            A copy of the database is committed to a GitHub repository you name - on a
            schedule, or when you press the button. Each backup replaces the last one at
            <code>@BackupWorker.RepositoryPath</code> and the repository's history keeps the
            rest; a database that has not changed makes no commit. Nothing comes back on
            its own: restoring a backup is putting the file back by hand.
        </p>

        <TextField Bare="true"
                   LabelCssClass="setting__label"
                   InputCssClass="@SettingsScreenContext.SettingInputClass(_storageRepositoryError)"
                   Label="Repository"
                   Id="storage-repository"
                   data-testid="storage-repository-input"
                   spellcheck="false"
                   autocomplete="off"
                   Placeholder="owner/repo"
                   aria-describedby="storage-repository-status"
                   Value="@_draftStorageRepository"
                   ValueChanged="value => _draftStorageRepository = value"
                   @onchange="ApplyStorageRepository"
                   @onkeydown="OnStorageRepositoryKeyDown" />

        <p class="setting__status" id="storage-repository-status" data-testid="storage-repository-status" role="status">
            @if (_storageRepositoryError is not null)
            {
                <span class="setting__status--error">@_storageRepositoryError</span>
            }
            else if (Store.RootRepository is { } storageRepository)
            {
                <span>Backs up to <code>@storageRepository.FullName</code>, using the GitHub account this machine is signed in with.</span>
            }
            else
            {
                <span>No backup repository configured.</span>
            }
        </p>

        @* A cadence picked from three, which is the shape SelectField
           exists for; the day and the time are shown only when the
           cadence gives them something to decide. Form-shaped, the way
           the account credential select is, because a bare caption
           beside bordered inputs does not read as a control. *@
        <SelectField Form="true"
                     Label="Schedule"
                     Id="storage-backup-cadence"
                     TestId="storage-backup-cadence"
                     LabelCssClass="setting__label"
                     SelectCssClass="setting__input"
                     CurrentValue="@Store.BackupSchedule.Cadence.ToString()"
                     Options="@BackupCadenceOptions"
                     OnChanged="ApplyBackupCadence" />

        @if (Store.BackupSchedule.Cadence is BackupCadence.Weekly)
        {
            <SelectField Form="true"
                         Label="On"
                         Id="storage-backup-day"
                         TestId="storage-backup-day"
                         LabelCssClass="setting__label"
                         SelectCssClass="setting__input"
                         CurrentValue="@Store.BackupSchedule.Day.ToString()"
                         Options="@BackupDayOptions"
                         OnChanged="ApplyBackupDay" />
        }

        @if (Store.BackupSchedule.IsScheduled)
        {
            @* Committed from the change event's own value rather than
               through ValueChanged, for the reason the Dashboard's
               working-week rows give: a time input raises empty input
               events while a time is half typed. *@
            <TextField Bare="true"
                       LabelCssClass="setting__label"
                       InputCssClass="@SettingsScreenContext.SettingInputClass(_backupScheduleError)"
                       Label="At"
                       Id="storage-backup-time"
                       data-testid="storage-backup-time"
                       Type="time"
                       autocomplete="off"
                       aria-describedby="storage-backup-status"
                       Value="@BackupTimeDraft"
                       @onchange="ApplyBackupTime"
                       @onkeydown="OnBackupTimeKeyDown" />
        }

        <div class="setting__actions">
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.None"
                       data-testid="clear-storage-repository-button"
                       Disabled="@(Store.RootRepository is null)"
                       OnClick="ClearStorageRepository">
                Clear backup repository
            </AppButton>
        </div>

        @if (_backup is not null)
        {
            <p class="setting__note">
                What the last backup did is kept in <code>@_backup.StorePath</code>, beside the
                app's other per-user files rather than in the backlog folder.
            </p>
        }
        </ChildContent>
    </SettingsCard>
</div>

@* The person's Brain (local ADR 0026): one folder outside every
   repository, picked here once. Below the two columns rather than in
   either, because it is neither the backlog's folder nor its backup -
   moving the backlog never moves the Brain. Drawn only where the host
   composes the Brain's setting, so a host without one still renders
   this screen. Until a folder is set the Brain chip reads "Set up your
   Brain" and its tooltip points here; the Devbook pane's first-use
   prompt links here (BrainSetup.SettingsHref). *@
@if (_brainSettings is not null && _brainSetup is not null)
{
    <BrainFolderCard Id="@BrainSetup.CardId"
                     TestId="brain-folder-card"
                     Folder="@_draftBrainFolder"
                     FolderChanged="value => _draftBrainFolder = value"
                     OnCommit="ApplyBrainFolder"
                     Status="@_brainStatus"
                     CanBrowse="@(_brainPicker is not null)"
                     Browsing="@_browsingBrainFolder"
                     OnBrowse="BrowseBrainFolderAsync"
                     OnCreateConventions="CreateBrainConventions"
                     Error="@_brainError"
                     Message="@_brainMessage" />
}

@code {
    private string _draftPath = string.Empty;
    private string? _error;
    private bool _moved;
    private bool _copied;
    private string _previousRoot = string.Empty;
    private string _draftStorageRepository = string.Empty;
    private string? _storageRepositoryError;
    private string? _backupScheduleError;
    private string _draftDevbookCacheDirectory = string.Empty;
    private string? _devbookCacheError;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        _log = Services.GetService<ILoggerFactory>()?.CreateLogger(GetType());
        _time = Services.GetService<TimeProvider>() ?? TimeProvider.System;
        _backup = ResolveBackup();

        // The worker outlives this screen, and what it does while somebody is
        // here has to reach the page, because otherwise the line below the
        // button never moves.
        if (_backup is not null) _backup.Changed += OnBackupChanged;

        _draftPath = Store.RootDirectory;
        _draftStorageRepository = Store.RootRepository?.ToLine() ?? string.Empty;

        // Blank while it is the default, so the placeholder is what explains
        // where snapshots go rather than a path nobody typed.
        _draftDevbookCacheDirectory = Store.IsDefaultDevbookCacheDirectory ? string.Empty : Store.DevbookCacheDirectory;
        InitializeBrain();
    }

    // ---- Headline and figures ---------------------------------------------

    /// <summary>The clock the relative times are read against: the host's when it
    /// composes one, so a test can hold it still.</summary>
    private TimeProvider _time = TimeProvider.System;

    /// <summary>The last backup that reached the repository, or null while none
    /// has - or while the host composes no worker to ask.</summary>
    private DateTimeOffset? LastBackupAt => _backup?.Last.SucceededAt;

    /// <summary>How many daily copies the repository's history keeps. At least
    /// one once a backup has worked: a state file written before the worker
    /// counted copies reads zero, and the copy it made is still there.</summary>
    private int DailyCopies => LastBackupAt is null
        ? 0
        : Math.Max(1, _backup?.Last.DailyCopies ?? 0);

    /// <summary>The page's headline: whether the backlog is safe, said first.</summary>
    private string Headline => LastBackupAt is { } at
        ? $"Backed up {Ago(at)} — {DailyCopies} daily {(DailyCopies == 1 ? "copy" : "copies")} kept."
        : "Not backed up yet.";

    private string LastBackupFigure => LastBackupAt is { } at ? Ago(at) : "Never";

    /// <summary>How long ago <paramref name="at"/> was, in the words the In
    /// progress list uses for the same question.</summary>
    private string Ago(DateTimeOffset at)
    {
        var elapsed = _time.GetUtcNow() - at;

        if (elapsed < TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1)) return "just now";
        if (elapsed < TimeSpan.FromHours(1)) return $"{(int)elapsed.TotalMinutes} min ago";
        if (elapsed < TimeSpan.FromDays(1)) return $"{(int)elapsed.TotalHours} h ago";

        var days = (int)elapsed.TotalDays;
        return days == 1 ? "yesterday" : $"{days} days ago";
    }

    // ---- Storage folder -----------------------------------------------------

    private bool _browsingStoragePath;

    /// <summary>Puts the picked folder in the field and commits nothing: Move and
    /// Switch are still the two ways to apply it, and which one is the reader's
    /// choice. The Brain card's picker, because a folder picker is a folder
    /// picker.</summary>
    private async Task BrowseStoragePathAsync()
    {
        if (_brainPicker is null || _browsingStoragePath) return;

        _browsingStoragePath = true;
        try
        {
            var picked = await _brainPicker.PickFolderAsync(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_draftPath) ? null : _draftPath);
            if (picked is not null) _draftPath = picked;
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is not OperationCanceledException)
        {
            _error = $"Couldn't open the folder picker: {ex.Message}";
        }
        finally
        {
            _browsingStoragePath = false;
        }
    }

    private async Task OnKeyDownAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Enter") await ApplyAsync();
        else if (e.Key == "Escape") _draftPath = Store.RootDirectory;
    }

    /// <summary>What "Move to this folder" and Enter in the field do: the
    /// backlog goes along to the folder typed in. The store copies before it
    /// repoints, and refuses a folder that already holds a backlog, so the
    /// worst a slip here can do is leave everything where it was and say why.</summary>
    private Task ApplyAsync() => ChangeRootAsync(RootAct.Move, _draftPath, Store.PrepareMoveRoot);

    /// <summary>The old behaviour, kept for opening a backlog that already
    /// lives in the folder: the app is pointed there and nothing is copied.</summary>
    private Task SwitchAsync() => ChangeRootAsync(RootAct.Switch, _draftPath, Store.PrepareUseRoot);

    /// <summary>Which of the three folder changes is under way, so its button
    /// says so and the other two wait.</summary>
    private enum RootAct
    {
        None,
        Move,
        Switch,
        Default,
    }

    private RootAct _rootAct;

    /// <summary>A root button's standing <c>disabled</c>: its own reason, unless
    /// it is the one running - a busy button keeps focus.</summary>
    private bool RootButtonDisabled(RootAct act, bool idleDisabled) => _rootAct != act && idleDisabled;

    /// <summary>Every root button says it is blocked while a folder change runs:
    /// the running one is busy, and the others drop a press
    /// (<see cref="ChangeRootAsync"/> ignores it) without leaving the tab order.
    /// Null when idle, so nothing overrides the button's own state.</summary>
    private string? RootButtonsBlocked => _rootAct is RootAct.None ? null : "true";

    /// <summary>
    /// Runs one folder change with its button busy from the press. A press - or
    /// Enter - while one is under way is ignored.
    /// <para>
    /// The store's two halves run on two threads. The preparation - resolving
    /// the folder and, for a move, copying the backlog into it - runs on the
    /// pool, so the window stays live however big the backlog is. The commit
    /// runs back here on the dispatcher, because it raises <c>RootChanged</c>
    /// and its listeners (the task list's reload among them) replace state the
    /// renderer reads.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ChangeRootAsync(RootAct act, string path, Func<string?, PreparedRoot> prepare)
    {
        if (_rootAct is not RootAct.None) return;

        _rootAct = act;
        try
        {
            var previous = Store.RootDirectory;
            var prepared = await Task.Run(() => prepare(path));
            var outcome = Store.CommitRoot(prepared);
            RecordRootChange(previous, outcome.Error, outcome.CopiedData);
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is IOException or UnauthorizedAccessException)
        {
            _error = $"Couldn't change the folder: {ex.Message}";
        }
        finally
        {
            _rootAct = RootAct.None;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// What the status line says after a commit. A commit that changed nothing
    /// and failed nothing leaves the last outcome standing: Enter pressed again
    /// on a field that now names the folder the app is in must not wipe "Moved"
    /// off the screen before anybody read which folder still holds the old copy.
    /// (It used to happen without a second key: the field committed on change
    /// as well, and Enter caused both.)
    /// </summary>
    private void RecordRootChange(string previous, string? error, bool copied)
    {
        _error = error;

        if (!string.Equals(previous, Store.RootDirectory, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
        {
            _moved = true;
            _copied = copied;
            _previousRoot = previous;
        }
        else if (error is not null)
        {
            _moved = false;
            _copied = false;
        }

        if (_error is null) _draftPath = Store.RootDirectory;
    }

    private Task MoveToDefaultAsync()
    {
        if (_rootAct is not RootAct.None) return Task.CompletedTask;

        _draftPath = Store.DefaultRootDirectory;
        return ChangeRootAsync(RootAct.Default, _draftPath, Store.PrepareMoveRoot);
    }

    /// <summary>Whether the field still names the folder the app is in, in
    /// which case switching has nothing to switch to.</summary>
    private bool IsCurrentRootDraft =>
        string.Equals(
            Path.TrimEndingDirectorySeparator(_draftPath.Trim()),
            Path.TrimEndingDirectorySeparator(Store.RootDirectory),
            StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

    private void ApplyStorageRepository()
    {
        _storageRepositoryError = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_draftStorageRepository)
            ? Store.ClearRepository()
            : Store.TrySetRepository(_draftStorageRepository);
        _draftStorageRepository = Store.RootRepository?.ToLine() ?? string.Empty;
    }

    private void ClearStorageRepository()
    {
        _storageRepositoryError = Store.ClearRepository();
        _draftStorageRepository = string.Empty;
    }

    private void OnStorageRepositoryKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Enter") ApplyStorageRepository();
        else if (e.Key == "Escape") _draftStorageRepository = Store.RootRepository?.ToLine() ?? string.Empty;
    }

    // ---- Backup schedule ---------------------------------------------------

    /// <summary>The worker, or null in a host that composes none.</summary>
    private BackupWorker? _backup;

    /// <summary>Optional, like the worker: a test rendering this screen should not
    /// have to compose a logging stack to reach a page it never opens.</summary>
    private ILogger? _log;

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> BackupCadenceOptions =
    [
        new(nameof(BackupCadence.Off), "Off"),
        new(nameof(BackupCadence.Daily), "Every day"),
        new(nameof(BackupCadence.Weekly), "Every week")
    ];

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> BackupDayOptions =
        [.. WorkingHours.Week.Select(day => new SelectorOption(day.ToString(), day.ToString()))];

    /// <summary>What the time field shows: the schedule's time as the input
    /// wants it, re-read on every render because the field commits on change
    /// and the store is what it commits to.</summary>
    private string BackupTimeDraft => Store.BackupSchedule.At.ToString("HH:mm", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);

    private void ApplyBackupCadence(string? value)
    {
        if (!Enum.TryParse<BackupCadence>(value, ignoreCase: true, out var cadence)) return;

        _backupScheduleError = Store.SetBackupSchedule(Store.BackupSchedule with { Cadence = cadence });
    }

    private void ApplyBackupDay(string? value)
    {
        if (!Enum.TryParse<DayOfWeek>(value, ignoreCase: true, out var day)) return;

        _backupScheduleError = Store.SetBackupSchedule(Store.BackupSchedule with { Day = day });
    }

    /// <summary>Commits the time from the change event's own value, for the
    /// reason the Dashboard's working-week editor gives: a time input raises empty
    /// input events while a time is half typed, and only the change event
    /// carries a whole one.</summary>
    private void ApplyBackupTime(ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        if (!TryReadTime(e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty, out var at))
        {
            _backupScheduleError = "Enter a time of day for the backup.";
            return;
        }

        _backupScheduleError = Store.SetBackupSchedule(Store.BackupSchedule with { At = at });
    }

    private void OnBackupTimeKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Escape") _backupScheduleError = null;
    }

    /// <summary>Asks the worker for a backup now, and returns. Not awaited, for
    /// the reason the Devices page's Sync now button is not: copying the
    /// database and uploading it is work a click handler should not hold the
    /// render loop for, and what happened arrives through the worker's
    /// <c>Changed</c>.</summary>
    private void BackUpNow() => _backup?.RequestBackup();

    private bool BackupStatusIsFailure =>
        _backupScheduleError is not null || (_backup is { IsRunning: false, Last.Error: not null });

    /// <summary>One sentence for the state of backups: the refusal of what was
    /// just typed first, then what the worker did last and when it will next,
    /// because a person reading this line is asking "is my backlog safe?" and
    /// the answer is the last backup, not the schedule.</summary>
    private string BackupStatusLine
    {
        get
        {
            if (_backupScheduleError is not null) return _backupScheduleError;
            if (_backup is null) return "Backups are not available in this build.";
            if (Store.RootRepository is null) return "Name a repository to back up to.";
            if (_backup.IsRunning) return "Backing up…";

            var last = _backup.Last;
            // Already local: the worker asks its clock for local time when it
            // arms, so converting again would shift it by the zone a second time.
            var next = _backup.NextDue is { } due
                ? $" Next backup {due:dddd d MMMM} at {due:HH:mm}."
                : Store.BackupSchedule.IsScheduled ? string.Empty : " Nothing is scheduled.";

            if (last.Error is not null)
            {
                var since = last.SucceededAt is { } ok ? $" The last backup that worked was {ok.ToLocalTime():d MMMM yyyy HH:mm}." : string.Empty;
                return $"The last backup failed: {last.Error}{since}{next}";
            }

            if (last.SucceededAt is { } at)
            {
                var did = last.Committed ? "a new copy was committed" : "nothing had changed";
                return $"Last backup {at.ToLocalTime():d MMMM yyyy HH:mm} - {did}.{next}";
            }

            return $"Never backed up.{next}";
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Resolved rather than injected, and resolved this way rather than
    /// with a plain <c>GetService</c>: both desktop heads compose one, a test
    /// rendering this page does not have to, and a worker that is registered but
    /// cannot be built throws rather than answering null.</summary>
    private BackupWorker? ResolveBackup()
    {
        try
        {
            return Services.GetService<BackupWorker>();
        }
        catch (InvalidOperationException ex)
        {
            _log?.LogInformation(ex, "Backup is not composed in this host, so the backup controls are not offered.");
            return null;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>A run ended or started on a thread that is not this one, or
    /// the schedule moved the next due time. The line under the button is what
    /// shows it.</summary>
    private void OnBackupChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    /// <summary>Seconds are accepted as well as minutes because a browser is
    /// entitled to send them, and refusing a time for being more precise than
    /// the field asked for is a rejection nobody could act on.</summary>
    private static bool TryReadTime(string text, out TimeOnly time) =>
        TimeOnly.TryParseExact(
            text,
            ["HH:mm", "HH:mm:ss"],
            CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
            DateTimeStyles.None,
            out time);


    /// <summary>What the store found the root to be inside, said as advice, or
    /// null when it found nothing - which renders no warning at all.</summary>
    private string? SyncedRootWarning => Store.SyncedRoot is null
        ? null
        : $"This folder is inside {Store.SyncedRoot.ProviderName}, which copies it between machines. It cannot merge the database, so entries and status edits can be lost - move the backlog to a folder on this disk.";

    /// <summary>The status line always, and the sync warning as well while there
    /// is one. Both are things the reader needs on reaching the field, and naming
    /// an element that is not rendered would describe the field with nothing.</summary>
    private string StoragePathDescribedBy => Store.SyncedRoot is null
        ? "storage-path-status"
        : "storage-path-status storage-path-sync-warning";

    /// <summary>Commits the snapshot folder. Blank returns it to the default,
    /// which is what clearing the field means everywhere else on this screen.</summary>
    private void ApplyDevbookCacheDirectory()
    {
        _devbookCacheError = Store.SetDevbookCacheDirectory(_draftDevbookCacheDirectory);
        _draftDevbookCacheDirectory = Store.IsDefaultDevbookCacheDirectory ? string.Empty : Store.DevbookCacheDirectory;
    }

    private void OnDevbookCacheDirectoryKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Enter") ApplyDevbookCacheDirectory();
        else if (e.Key == "Escape") _draftDevbookCacheDirectory = Store.IsDefaultDevbookCacheDirectory ? string.Empty : Store.DevbookCacheDirectory;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Lets go of everything that outlives a render.
    /// <para>
    /// The backup worker is not disposed here - it is a singleton, and it goes on
    /// running after this page closes, which is the whole point of it. What is let
    /// go of is the subscriptions: a page still on a singleton's event would be
    /// kept alive by it and would call <c>StateHasChanged</c> on a renderer that is
    /// gone.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    public void Dispose()
    {
        if (_brainSettings is not null) _brainSettings.Changed -= OnBrainSettingsChanged;
        if (_backup is not null) _backup.Changed -= OnBackupChanged;
    }
}
